Consider a social organization modeled as a multilayer network G = (V, E) in which each agent i ∈ V is endowed with a utility function U_i(x_i, x_{-i}; θ_i) depending on the agent's own state x_i, the states x_{-i} of its neighbors, and parameters θ_i. Agents update their states (and possibly their links) by best-response or noisy best-response dynamics that maximize U_i, which can induce changes in the topology E over time. Define the resilience R(G, t) of the organization as the ability of the macroscopic state X(t) = (x_i(t))_{i ∈ V} to remain in, or return to, a desired regime after exogenous perturbations.

A network intervention is specified by (i) a set D ⊆ V of driver nodes, (ii) a type τ of intervention (e.g., modification of parameters θ_i, injection of an external signal s_i(t) into the utility, or addition/removal of links incident to i ∈ D), and (iii) an intervention magnitude a ∈ ℝ^{|D|}_{≥0} subject to a budget constraint Σ_{i ∈ D} c_i(a_i) ≤ B.

Develop a mathematical framework that, given the model (G, {U_i}, dynamics) and a target resilience level R*, determines:
1. an optimal choice of driver nodes D*;
2. an optimal intervention type τ* and magnitude a*;
3. a model of how the control signal s_i(t) (or parameter change) modifies each U_i and the resulting local best-response decisions;
4. a forecast of how these local decisions propagate through the multilayer topology and dynamics so as to certify that R(G(t), t) ≥ R* for all t ≥ t_0 under admissible perturbations.

Prove existence and, where possible, uniqueness of such optimal interventions, and provide conditions on G, {U_i}, and the dynamics under which the resulting controlled system achieves the desired resilience.

